<body>

<header>
  <h1>Tiananmen Square, 1989</h1>
  <p>A timeline of events, April – June 1989</p>
</header>

<main>

  <div class="intro">
    <strong>Context:</strong> In the spring of 1989, students, intellectuals, and citizens gathered in
    Beijing's Tiananmen Square to mourn the death of reformist party leader Hu Yaobang and to call for
    political reform, an end to corruption, and greater democracy. The demonstrations grew over seven weeks
    and spread to cities across China. On the night of June 3–4, the government ordered the military to
    clear the square, resulting in a violent crackdown. Discussion of these events remains restricted in
    mainland China.
  </div>

  <h2>Timeline of Key Events</h2>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 15, 1989</span>
      <h3>Death of Hu Yaobang</h3>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, a former General Secretary of the Communist Party and a popular reformist, dies.
      Students begin gathering in Tiananmen Square to mourn him.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 17–18</span>
      <h3>Mourning turns to protest</h3>
      <p>Students from Beijing universities march to the square. Grief over Hu's death evolves into
      demands for democratic reform, freedom of the press, and an end to corruption.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 22</span>
      <h3>Memorial service &amp; mass gathering</h3>
      <p>On the day of Hu Yaobang's memorial service at the Great Hall of the People, an estimated
      100,000–200,000 students gather in the square. Student representatives attempt to deliver a
      petition to government officials.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 26</span>
      <h3>The People's Daily editorial</h3>
      <p>A <em>People's Daily</em> editorial, reflecting hardline views, denounces the protests as
      "turmoil" orchestrated by a small minority. Rather than quelling the movement, the editorial
      galvanizes larger demonstrations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 4</span>
      <h3>May Fourth anniversary marches</h3>
      <p>Tens of thousands march in Beijing on the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement.
      Similar demonstrations occur in dozens of Chinese cities.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 13</span>
      <h3>Hunger strike begins</h3>
      <p>More than 2,000 students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, raising the stakes and
      drawing widespread public sympathy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 15–18</span>
      <h3>Gorbachev's visit</h3>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing for the Sino-Soviet summit — the first such visit
      in three decades. The ongoing protests disrupt and embarrass the visit.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 19</span>
      <h3>Zhao Ziyang's plea</h3>
      <p>Party General Secretary Zhao Ziyang visits the square in the early morning hours and tearfully
      urges hunger strikers to end their fast. It is his last public appearance; he is soon removed
      from power for his sympathy toward the students.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 20</span>
      <h3>Martial law declared</h3>
      <p>Premier Li Peng declares martial law in Beijing. Troops begin moving toward the city center
      but are blocked for days by crowds of citizens and students.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 23–24</span>
      <h3>Citizens defend the square</h3>
      <p>Hundreds of thousands of Beijing residents — estimates reach roughly one million — march to
      protect the students and block army vehicles from reaching the square.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event crackdown">
      <span class="date">June 3 (evening)</span>
      <h3>Troops advance on Beijing</h3>
      <p>In the evening, soldiers armed with live ammunition begin moving into the city. Violent
      clashes erupt between troops and civilians around Beijing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event crackdown">
      <span class="date">June 4 (early morning)</span>
      <h3>The square is cleared</h3>
      <p>In the early hours, the military clears Tiananmen Square of the remaining demonstrators.
      Shooting continues across Beijing throughout the day. The government characterizes the crackdown
      as suppressing a "counter-revolutionary rebellion."</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">June 5</span>
      <h3>"Tank Man"</h3>
      <p>A lone, unidentified man briefly stands in front of a column of tanks on Chang'an Avenue,
      blocking their advance. The moment, captured by photographers, becomes one of the most iconic
      images of the 20th century. The man's identity and fate remain unknown.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">Aftermath</span>
      <h3>Arrests and repression</h3>
      <p>Thousands are arrested in the weeks that follow. Participants face trials, imprisonment, and
      "re-education." A nationwide crackdown on dissent follows, and the events remain officially
      censored in mainland China.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

  <div class="estimates">
    <h2>Estimated Number of Victims</h2>
    <p>The death toll remains <strong>deeply contested</strong>. The Chinese government has never released
    verifiable, comprehensive casualty figures, and estimates vary enormously depending on the source:</p>

    <table>
      <tr>
        <th>Source</th>
        <th>Estimate</th>
        <th>Notes</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>PRC government (official)</td>
        <td>~241 killed</td>
        <td>Official 1989 figures, including soldiers and people the government classifies as
        "rioters." Official accounts state no one was killed inside the square itself.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Chinese Red Cross (reported, 1989)</td>
        <td>~2,600 killed</td>
        <td>Widely reported by news agencies at the time as a Red Cross figure; the organization
        later denied issuing it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Western governments / intelligence (1989)</td>
        <td>Several hundred to a few thousand</td>
        <td>Estimates made in the immediate aftermath, based on hospital reports and eyewitness
        accounts.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Declassified British diplomatic cables (2017)</td>
        <td>At least 10,000</td>
        <td>A UK National Archives cable, declassified in 2017, cited a source close to China's
        State Council estimating at least 10,000 civilian deaths. This figure is disputed.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Independent researchers &amp; human rights groups</td>
        <td>Hundreds to low thousands</td>
        <td>Analyses of hospital records, morgue reports, and eyewitness testimony; no consensus
        figure exists.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <p class="caveat">Because the Chinese government has not permitted an independent investigation and
    continues to suppress information about the crackdown, the true number of deaths is unknown. The
    figures above are presented as a range of documented estimates, not as established fact.</p>
  </div>

</main>

<footer>
  <p>Compiled from publicly available reporting and historical records (news agencies, declassified
  government documents, and human rights organizations).<br>
  This page is a factual summary; figures relating to casualties are contested and presented with attribution.</p>
</footer>

</body>
</html>
```

A few notes on the choices I made:

- **Neutral, factual framing** — events are presented chronologically with widely accepted historical facts (dates, participants, sequence), which are not in dispute among historians.
- **Casualty figures handled carefully** — since the death toll is genuinely contested, I presented a table of estimates *with attribution and caveats* (official PRC figure, the reported-but-denied Red Cross figure, Western intelligence estimates, the 2017 declassified UK cable, and independent research), plus an explicit note that no verified number exists. I avoided asserting any single figure as fact.
- **Context included** — a brief note that discussion of the crackdown is restricted in mainland China, since that's relevant context for readers.
- **Design** — a single self-contained file with a clean vertical timeline, muted historical palette, a highlighted treatment for the June 3–4 crackdown entries, and a responsive layout that works on mobile.

