<title>Beijing Spring 1989 — A Timeline (April–June)</title>
<style>
  :root{
    --bg:#14161a;
    --bg-soft:#1c1f26;
    --card:#22262e;
    --ink:#e8e6e1;
    --muted:#9a978f;
    --accent:#c8a24a;
    --accent-soft:#e3c98a;
    --line:#34383f;
    --red:#a8433c;
  }
  *{margin:0;padding:0;box-sizing:border-box;}
  html{scroll-behavior:smooth;}
  body{
    background:var(--bg);
    color:var(--ink);
    font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;
    line-height:1.7;
    -webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased;
  }
  .wrap{max-width:820px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 24px;}

  header{
    padding:88px 0 40px;
    text-align:center;
    border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .kicker{
    font-family:'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;
    font-size:12px;
    letter-spacing:.35em;
    text-transform:uppercase;
    color:var(--accent);
    margin-bottom:18px;
  }
  h1{
    font-size:clamp(30px,5vw,46px);
    font-weight:normal;
    letter-spacing:.02em;
    margin-bottom:14px;
  }
  .subtitle{
    color:var(--muted);
    font-size:17px;
    max-width:600px;
    margin:0 auto;
  }

  .intro{
    background:var(--bg-soft);
    border:1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius:10px;
    padding:26px 30px;
    margin:48px 0 12px;
    font-size:16px;
    color:#cfccc4;
  }
  .intro strong{color:var(--accent-soft);font-weight:normal;}

  /* ---------- Timeline ---------- */
  .timeline{
    position:relative;
    margin:56px 0 0;
    padding-left:34px;
  }
  .timeline::before{
    content:"";
    position:absolute;
    left:8px;top:6px;bottom:6px;
    width:2px;
    background:linear-gradient(to bottom,var(--accent),var(--red));
    opacity:.55;
  }
  .event{
    position:relative;
    background:var(--card);
    border:1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius:10px;
    padding:24px 28px;
    margin-bottom:26px;
    transition:transform .25s ease,border-color .25s ease;
  }
  .event:hover{transform:translateX(6px);border-color:#4a4f58;}
  .event::before{
    content:"";
    position:absolute;
    left:-32px;top:30px;
    width:12px;height:12px;
    border-radius:50%;
    background:var(--bg);
    border:3px solid var(--accent);
  }
  .event.major::before{border-color:var(--red);}
  .date{
    font-family:'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;
    font-size:12px;
    letter-spacing:.22em;
    text-transform:uppercase;
    color:var(--accent);
    margin-bottom:8px;
  }
  .event.major .date{color:#d4796f;}
  .event h2{
    font-size:21px;
    font-weight:normal;
    margin-bottom:10px;
    letter-spacing:.01em;
  }
  .event p{
    font-size:15.5px;
    color:#c4c1b9;
  }
  .tag{
    display:inline-block;
    font-family:'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;
    font-size:10.5px;
    letter-spacing:.14em;
    text-transform:uppercase;
    padding:3px 10px;
    border-radius:20px;
    border:1px solid var(--line);
    color:var(--muted);
    margin-left:10px;
    vertical-align:2px;
  }
  .tag.hot{border-color:var(--red);color:#d4796f;}

  /* ---------- Casualties ---------- */
  .casualties{
    margin:64px 0 0;
    border:1px solid var(--line);
    border-left:4px solid var(--red);
    border-radius:10px;
    background:var(--bg-soft);
    padding:32px 34px;
  }
  .casualties h2{
    font-size:24px;
    font-weight:normal;
    margin-bottom:16px;
    color:var(--accent-soft);
  }
  .casualties p{font-size:15.5px;color:#c4c1b9;margin-bottom:14px;}
  .figures{
    display:grid;
    grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(200px,1fr));
    gap:16px;
    margin:22px 0 8px;
  }
  .fig{
    background:var(--card);
    border:1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius:8px;
    padding:16px 18px;
  }
  .fig .num{
    font-size:26px;
    color:var(--ink);
    display:block;
    margin-bottom:4px;
  }
  .fig .src{
    font-family:'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;
    font-size:12px;
    color:var(--muted);
    line-height:1.5;
    display:block;
  }
  .disclaimer{
    font-size:13.5px;
    color:var(--muted);
    font-style:italic;
    margin-top:14px;
  }

  footer{
    margin:72px 0 48px;
    padding-top:28px;
    border-top:1px solid var(--line);
    text-align:center;
    color:var(--muted);
    font-size:13.5px;
  }
  footer p{max-width:640px;margin:0 auto 8px;}

  @media (max-width:560px){
    .event{padding:20px;}
    .casualties{padding:24px 20px;}
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <div class="kicker">A Chronology</div>
    <h1>The Beijing Spring, 1989</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A timeline of the pro-democracy demonstrations in Tiananmen Square and the military crackdown of June 3–4, as the events unfolded from April to June.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="intro">
    In the spring of 1989, students, workers, and intellectuals gathered in Beijing's Tiananmen Square — initially to mourn a reformist Communist Party leader, and later to call for political reform, an end to corruption, and greater press freedom. The movement grew to involve hundreds of thousands of people in Beijing and sympathy protests in dozens of Chinese cities, before the government declared martial law and sent in the army. <strong>Casualty figures remain disputed and cannot be independently verified.</strong>
  </div>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 15, 1989</div>
      <h2>Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, the former General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party who had been removed from power in 1987 for his sympathy toward student protesters, dies of a heart attack. Students at Beijing universities begin gathering to mourn him — mourning quickly turns into political expression.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 18 – 22</div>
      <h2>First gatherings in the Square</h2>
      <p>Tens of thousands of students march to Tiananmen Square, laying wreaths and posting wall posters. Their demands expand from mourning to calls for democracy, freedom of the press, and an end to official corruption. On the night of April 18, thousands attempt to enter the Xinhua Gate of Zhongnanhai, the leadership compound, and clash with police.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 22</div>
      <h2>Memorial service</h2>
      <p>On the day of Hu Yaobang's memorial service, an estimated 100,000 students gather in the square despite official warnings. Student representatives present a petition to the government and are refused a meeting with senior leaders.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 26</div>
      <h2>The <em>People's Daily</em> editorial</h2>
      <p>A front-page editorial in the party newspaper <em>People's Daily</em> denounces the protests as a "planned conspiracy" and "turmoil" to be firmly opposed. Rather than intimidating the movement, the editorial enrages it — the next day, far larger crowds take to the streets.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 4</div>
      <h2>May Fourth anniversary</h2>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, tens of thousands of students march again. Sympathy demonstrations spread to Shanghai, Guangzhou, Wuhan, and many other cities. Journalists at state media begin openly sympathizing with the protesters.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 13</div>
      <h2>Hunger strike begins</h2>
      <p>More than a thousand students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, demanding formal dialogue with the government. The strike draws enormous public sympathy, and by mid-May the square regularly hosts crowds of several hundred thousand.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 15 – 18</div>
      <h2>Gorbachev's visit</h2>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev arrives in Beijing for the first Sino-Soviet summit in three decades. The prodemocracy protests overshadow the diplomatic events and embarrass the Chinese leadership on the world stage. Hundreds of thousands fill the square as his visit ends.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 19 – 20</div>
      <h2>Martial law declared</h2>
      <p>Party leaders agree to impose martial law. On the evening of May 19, Premier Li Peng meets student representatives in a televised encounter that ends in deadlock. Martial law is formally announced on May 20. Tens of thousands of unarmed Beijing residents pour onto the streets and block army trucks attempting to advance into the city center.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 23 – 30</div>
      <h2>Mass mobilization</h2>
      <p>Protesters and residents continue to obstruct the army's advance with barricades and human cordons. On May 30, students unveil the 10-meter "Goddess of Democracy" statue in the square, facing Mao Zedong's portrait on Tiananmen Gate. On May 28, an estimated one million people march in Beijing in support of the students.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event major">
      <div class="date">June 3 – 4 <span class="tag hot">Crackdown</span></div>
      <h2>The army moves in</h2>
      <p>In the evening of June 3, martial-law troops — many from outside Beijing — advance on the city with armored vehicles and live ammunition. Violent clashes erupt across western Beijing as soldiers force their way toward the square. In the early hours of June 4, troops clear Tiananmen Square itself. Fighting continues in surrounding streets into the morning. The world watches as the square is emptied by force.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 5</div>
      <h2>"Tank Man"</h2>
      <p>On Chang'an Avenue, a lone man in a white shirt briefly stands in front of a column of tanks, halting it before stepping aside. The image — captured by several photographers — becomes one of the most famous photographs of the 20th century. The man's identity and fate remain unknown.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 9</div>
      <h2>Aftermath</h2>
      <p>Deng Xiaoping praises the military's actions in a televised address, and a nationwide crackdown follows: thousands of suspected participants are arrested, student leaders are hunted or flee abroad, and the party launches a campaign of political "rectification." Protests in other cities are suppressed in the following days and weeks.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

  <section class="casualties">
    <h2>Estimated number of victims</h2>
    <p>The death toll of the June 3–4 crackdown is <strong>officially disputed and has never been independently verified</strong>. No complete, authoritative list of the dead has ever been published, and the Chinese government has never released a full accounting.</p>
    <div class="figures">
      <div class="fig">
        <span class="num">~200 – 300</span>
        <span class="src">Official Chinese government figure for deaths in Beijing, including soldiers and civilians (stated in 1989 and reiterated in later official accounts).</span>
      </div>
      <div class="fig">
        <span class="num">Several hundred to ~3,000</span>
        <span class="src">Range of estimates from Western diplomats, journalists, and human rights organizations at the time and in subsequent years.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="fig">
        <span class="num">~2,600</span>
        <span class="src">Figure reportedly given by the Chinese Red Cross on the night of June 4, later denied by the organization under pressure; cited by some Western officials.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="fig">
        <span class="num">"At least 10,000"</span>
        <span class="src">Figure attributed to Chinese military intelligence in a British diplomatic cable declassified in 2017; widely reported but contested by other analysts.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="disclaimer">These figures vary enormously because the Chinese government has restricted access to records, hospitals, and witnesses. Most independent researchers consider the true toll impossible to establish precisely; it is generally agreed that the majority of the dead were ordinary Beijing residents and students, with soldiers also among the casualties.</p>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p>This page is a neutral historical summary. For further reading, consult academic works on the 1989 protests, contemporary journalism, and the declassified documents released by governments and archives in the years since.</p>
    <p>Remembering the events of 1989 remains sensitive in China, where public discussion is restricted. The Mothers of Tiananmen — relatives of the victims — have campaigned for decades for an official accounting of the dead.</p>
  </footer>

</div>

</body>
</html>
```

A few notes on the page:

- **Structure**: A single vertical timeline runs from Hu Yaobang's death (April 15) through the crackdown (June 3–4) to the aftermath (June 9), with the two most consequential events — the crackdown and its date — visually emphasized in red.
- **Casualty figures**: Since the death toll is genuinely disputed, the page presents a panel of the main estimates (official Chinese figure, Red Cross report, Western estimates, and the declassified British cable) side by side, with an explicit disclaimer that no figure can be independently verified. This is the historically honest way to present it.
- **Design**: A restrained dark theme with gold/red accents suits the solemn subject matter; it's fully responsive and uses no external dependencies — just open the file in any browser.

