<body>
<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <h1>Tiananmen Square, 1989</h1>
    <p class="sub">A timeline of the pro-democracy demonstrations and the military crackdown, April–June 1989</p>
  </header>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 15, 1989</span>
      <h2>Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
      <p>The popular former General Secretary of the Communist Party, a reformist figure, dies of a heart attack. Students begin gathering in Beijing to mourn him, and the gatherings quickly turn into calls for political reform.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 18–22</span>
      <h2>Protests spread to Tiananmen Square</h2>
      <p>Tens of thousands of students march to Tiananmen Square, occupying the plaza. Similar demonstrations erupt in Shanghai, Xi'an, and other cities. Protesters demand an end to corruption and greater press freedom.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 26</span>
      <h2>"April 26 Editorial"</h2>
      <p>The state-run <em>People's Daily</em> publishes an editorial denouncing the protests as a "turmoil" orchestrated by a small group. The editorial backfires, galvanizing larger demonstrations the following day.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 27</span>
      <h2>Mass student march</h2>
      <p>An estimated 200,000 students and citizens march through Beijing in defiance of the editorial — one of the largest demonstrations of the movement.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 4</span>
      <h2>May Fourth anniversary</h2>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, hundreds of thousands gather in the square. Sympathy strikes and demonstrations spread to dozens of Chinese cities.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 13</span>
      <h2>Hunger strike begins</h2>
      <p>Students begin a mass hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, drawing enormous public sympathy. By mid-May, over a million people are estimated to have gathered in Beijing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 15–18</span>
      <h2>Gorbachev visit</h2>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing for a Sino-Soviet summit — the first in 30 years. Protesters' presence overshadows the visit, embarrassing the Chinese leadership on the world stage.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 19</span>
      <h2>Martial law declared</h2>
      <p>Party leaders declare martial law. General Secretary Zhao Ziyang pleads with students in the square to end the occupation; he is soon removed from power. Citizens erect barricades to block army movements into the city.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 20</span>
      <h2>Troops advance on Beijing</h2>
      <p>Military units move toward the city center but are slowed or turned back by large crowds of civilians blocking the roads.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event major">
      <span class="date">June 3–4, 1989</span>
      <h2>Military crackdown</h2>
      <p>In the night of June 3 and early hours of June 4, the People's Liberation Army moves into central Beijing with tanks and armed troops. Gunfire is reported in and around Tiananmen Square as soldiers clear the plaza. Students withdraw from the square in the early morning of June 4.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">June 5</span>
      <h2>"Tank Man"</h2>
      <p>A lone man in a white shirt stands in front of a column of tanks on Chang'an Avenue, blocking their advance. The moment is captured by international journalists and becomes an enduring image of the events.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">Aftermath</span>
      <h2>Arrests and suppression</h2>
      <p>In the weeks that follow, authorities arrest thousands of suspected participants, and many student leaders are imprisoned or sent into exile. Discussion of the events is heavily censored in mainland China.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

  <section class="casualties">
    <h2>Estimated number of victims</h2>
    <p style="font-size:0.92rem; margin-bottom:1rem;">
      No official, verifiable death toll has ever been released by the Chinese government, and all figures remain estimates that vary widely by source.
    </p>
    <table>
      <tr>
        <th>Source</th>
        <th>Estimated deaths</th>
        <th>Notes</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Chinese government (official)</td>
        <td>~200–300</td>
        <td>Stated figure of 241 killed (including soldiers) and thousands wounded, announced June 1989; widely considered a lower bound.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Chinese Red Cross (unofficial, 1989)</td>
        <td>~2,600</td>
        <td>Figure reportedly given during the crackdown, later denied by the organization under pressure; often cited by human rights groups.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Western governments &amp; media (1989)</td>
        <td>Several hundred to ~3,000</td>
        <td>Early reports, including from the U.S. Embassy, cited figures in the low thousands; these were based on hospital reports and eyewitness accounts.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Declassified documents (e.g., UK National Archives, 2017)</td>
        <td>10,000+ (disputed)</td>
        <td>A formerly secret British diplomatic cable cited a Chinese source claiming at least 10,000 civilian deaths; historians treat this as unverified and likely exaggerated.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Scholarly consensus (e.g., Columbia, China historians)</td>
        <td>Hundreds to low thousands</td>
        <td>Most academic work concludes deaths were in the hundreds, concentrated on the night of June 3–4, though the exact number is unknowable.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p><strong>Note on sources:</strong> This timeline is compiled from established historical accounts, including Western journalism of the period, declassified diplomatic cables, and academic studies. Casualty figures are estimates; the true number of deaths remains unknown and disputed.</p>
    <p><strong>Context:</strong> The events of June 4, 1989 are subject to censorship in mainland China. Organizations such as the Tiananmen Mothers — relatives of the victims — continue to seek accountability and an accurate accounting.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem; font-style:italic;">For further reading: <em>Tiananmen Diary</em> and eyewitness collections by journalists present in Beijing; the National Security Archive (George Washington University); Amnesty International reports.</p>
  </footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
```

**What's included:**

- **Vertical timeline** with 11 dated events from Hu Yaobang's death (April 15) through the crackdown (June 3–4) and its aftermath, with the crackdown highlighted as the pivotal "major" event.
- **Casualties table** presenting the range of estimates with attribution: the official Chinese figure (~241), the disputed Red Cross figure (~2,600), Western estimates, the controversial declassified 10,000+ claim (flagged as disputed), and the scholarly consensus — with a clear note that no verifiable official toll exists.
- **Sourcing footer** pointing to archives and human rights organizations, plus a note about censorship and the Tiananmen Mothers.
- **Design:** a clean, print-friendly serif layout with a muted red accent, responsive for mobile, fully self-contained (no external dependencies).

