<header class="site">
  <h1>Tiananmen Square, 1989</h1>
  <p>A factual timeline of the pro-democracy demonstrations in Beijing and the military crackdown of June 3–4, 1989.</p>
</header>

<main>

  <div class="intro">
    <strong>Context:</strong> In the spring of 1989, students, workers, and other citizens gathered in
    Beijing's Tiananmen Square to mourn the death of reformist Communist Party leader Hu Yaobang and to
    call for political reform, an end to corruption, and greater freedom of the press. The demonstrations
    grew over seven weeks before the government ordered the People's Liberation Army to clear the square
    by force on the night of June 3–4.
  </div>

  <div class="timeline" id="timeline">
    <div class="progress" id="progress"></div>

    <article class="event">
      <div class="date">April 15, 1989</div>
      <h2>Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, a former General Secretary of the Communist Party known for his reformist views,
      dies of a heart attack. Students begin gathering in Tiananmen Square to mourn him, and the
      gatherings quickly turn into calls for political change.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="event">
      <div class="date">April 18 – 22</div>
      <h2>Demonstrations grow</h2>
      <p>Thousands of students march to the square, joined by workers and residents. On April 22,
      the day of Hu's memorial service, roughly 100,000 students attempt to enter the Great Hall of
      the People to present petitions to Premier Li Peng, who declines to meet them.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="event">
      <div class="date">April 26</div>
      <h2>The "April 26 Editorial"</h2>
      <p>A <em>People's Daily</em> editorial denounces the protests as a "planned conspiracy" and
      "turmoil." Rather than intimidating the movement, the editorial galvanizes it — hundreds of
      thousands march in Beijing the following days.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="event">
      <div class="date">Early May</div>
      <h2>Citywide support</h2>
      <p>Students in dozens of Chinese cities demonstrate in solidarity. On May 4, the 70th
      anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, large marches take place across the country.
      Calls grow for dialogue with the government and for democratic reforms.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="event">
      <div class="date">May 13</div>
      <h2>Hunger strike begins</h2>
      <p>More than a thousand students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, demanding formal
      talks with the government. The protest draws enormous public sympathy; at its peak, estimates
      of supporters in the square reach around one million. Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits
      Beijing on May 15 for a Sino-Soviet summit.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="event">
      <div class="date">May 19</div>
      <h2>Zhao Ziyang pleads with students</h2>
      <p>Party General Secretary Zhao Ziyang visits the square in the early hours, tearfully urging
      students to end the hunger strike. The same day, the government declares martial law in parts
      of Beijing, effective May 20.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="event">
      <div class="date">May 20 – June 2</div>
      <h2>Civilians block the troops</h2>
      <p>Tens of thousands of Beijing residents — students, workers, and ordinary citizens — surround
      military vehicles and prevent troops from reaching the city center. Barricades are erected
      around the square, and sympathy for the students spreads widely.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="event crackdown">
      <div class="date">June 3 – 4</div>
      <h2>The crackdown</h2>
      <p>In the evening of June 3, the government orders the army to clear Tiananmen Square by
      morning. Troops open fire on crowds and advance on the square through the night. By the
      early hours of June 4, soldiers have secured the square; sporadic gunfire continues in
      surrounding neighborhoods into June 4.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="event">
      <div class="date">June 5</div>
      <h2>"Tank Man" and aftermath</h2>
      <p>A lone man famously stands in front of a column of tanks on Chang'an Avenue — an image that
      becomes one of the most recognized photographs of the 20th century. In the following weeks,
      authorities arrest thousands of suspected participants; many are imprisoned, and some were
      executed. The events remain heavily censored in mainland China.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <section class="victims">
    <h2>Estimated number of victims</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Source</th>
          <th>Estimate</th>
          <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Chinese government (1989)</td>
          <td>~300 killed</td>
          <td>Official figure given by State Council spokesman Yuan Mu; described as including
          soldiers, police, and civilians.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Chinese Red Cross (unofficial, 1989)</td>
          <td>~2,600 killed</td>
          <td>Figure reported at the time but later denied by the organization; widely cited
          by human rights groups.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Western governments &amp; intelligence, 1989</td>
          <td>~500 – 3,000 killed</td>
          <td>Estimates made in the weeks after the crackdown, based on hospital and eyewitness
          reports; ranges vary considerably.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Declassified UK diplomatic cable (2017)</td>
          <td>up to ~10,000 killed</td>
          <td>Cited a Chinese internal source; the figure is disputed and far above most other
          estimates.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">The exact death toll remains unknown and disputed. No official, independently
    verified count exists. Most scholarly and human rights assessments place the number of
    civilian deaths in the hundreds to low thousands, with thousands more injured and arrested.</p>
  </section>

</main>

<footer class="site">
  <p>Compiled for educational purposes from encyclopedic and journalistic sources
  (e.g., Encyclopaedia Britannica, BBC, Reuters archives, Human Rights Watch).
  Estimates of casualties vary widely and are presented with attribution.</p>
</footer>

<script>
  // Fade in each event as it enters the viewport
  const events = document.querySelectorAll('.event');
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach(entry => {
      if (entry.isIntersecting) {
        entry.target.classList.add('visible');
        observer.unobserve(entry.target);
      }
    });
  }, { threshold: 0.15 });
  events.forEach(el => observer.observe(el));

  // Progress line that fills as the user scrolls the timeline
  const timeline = document.getElementById('timeline');
  const progress = document.getElementById('progress');

  function updateProgress() {
    const rect = timeline.getBoundingClientRect();
    const total = rect.height - window.innerHeight * 0.5;
    const scrolled = Math.min(Math.max(-rect.top + window.innerHeight * 0.5, 0), total);
    progress.style.height = (scrolled / total * 100) + '%';
  }
  window.addEventListener('scroll', updateProgress, { passive: true });
  window.addEventListener('resize', updateProgress);
  updateProgress();
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

**What's included:**

- **Timeline of key dates** — April 15 (Hu Yaobang's death) through June 5 ("Tank Man"), with a short factual description for each event. The June 3–4 crackdown entry is visually emphasized.
- **Victims section** — a sourced table of death-toll estimates (Chinese government ~300; unofficial Red Cross ~2,600; Western estimates ~500–3,000; a disputed declassified UK figure up to ~10,000), with an explicit note that no independently verified count exists. I deliberately presented the range with attribution rather than a single number, since the figure is genuinely disputed.
- **Design & behavior** — a clean, respectful editorial style (serif type, muted red accent), a vertical timeline whose red progress line fills as you scroll, and events that fade in via `IntersectionObserver`. No external dependencies; fully responsive.

**A note on sourcing:** this topic is censored in mainland China, so the figures above come from international encyclopedias, journalism, and human rights organizations. If you'd like, I can add a sources list with links, or adjust the tone/length of any entry.

