<body>
<main>

  <header>
    <h1>Tiananmen Square, 1989</h1>
    <p class="sub">A timeline of the pro-democracy protests and crackdown, April–June 1989</p>
  </header>

  <div class="note">
    Content note: This page summarizes a well-documented historical event for educational purposes.
    Casualty figures remain disputed; estimates below are attributed and should be read as ranges, not certainties.
  </div>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 15, 1989</span>
      <h2>Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, the reform-minded former General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party, dies of a heart attack. Students begin gathering in Beijing to mourn him, viewing him as a symbol of political reform.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 18–22</span>
      <h2>Protests spread to Tiananmen Square</h2>
      <p>Mourning gatherings evolve into demonstrations calling for government accountability, press freedom, and an end to corruption. On April 22, the day of Hu's funeral, an estimated 100,000 people assemble in the square.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">April 26</span>
      <h2>People's Daily editorial</h2>
      <p>A People's Daily editorial denounces the demonstrations as "turmoil" orchestrated by a small group. The editorial hardens positions on both sides and draws larger crowds to the square.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 4</span>
      <h2>May Fourth anniversary demonstrations</h2>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, students across China hold marches. Demonstrations in Beijing swell, with estimates of up to a million people in and around the square at the peak.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 13</span>
      <h2>Hunger strike begins</h2>
      <p>More than 2,000 students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, demanding dialogue with the government. Support from Beijing citizens grows rapidly.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 15–18</span>
      <h2>Gorbachev visit</h2>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing for the Sino-Soviet summit — the first such visit in 30 years. The high-profile event embarrasses Chinese leadership as protesters dominate international coverage.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 19</span>
      <h2>Martial law declared</h2>
      <p>Party leaders declare martial law. Premier Li Peng addresses the nation; Party General Secretary Zhao Ziyang makes a televised plea to students to end the hunger strike. Zhao is soon removed from power.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 20 – June 2</span>
      <h2>Citizens block the troops</h2>
      <p>Hundreds of thousands of Beijing residents surround military vehicles and prevent troops from reaching the city center. A festive, defiant atmosphere persists in the square, with students maintaining an occupation camp.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">May 30</span>
      <h2>"Goddess of Democracy" erected</h2>
      <p>Art students unveil a 10-meter statue modeled on the Statue of Liberty in the square, facing Mao Zedong's portrait on Tiananmen Gate. It becomes an iconic symbol of the movement.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event crackdown">
      <span class="date">June 3–4 (night)</span>
      <h2>The crackdown</h2>
      <p>In the evening of June 3, troops with live ammunition advance on Beijing. Violent clashes erupt along the approach roads as residents try to stop them. In the early hours of June 4, soldiers clear Tiananmen Square itself; gunfire is reported across the city through the night and into the morning.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event crackdown">
      <span class="date">June 5</span>
      <h2>"Tank Man"</h2>
      <p>A lone man in a white shirt famously stands in front of a column of tanks on Chang'an Avenue, blocking their advance. The image, captured by photographers, becomes one of the most recognizable photographs of the 20th century. The man's identity and fate remain unknown.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <span class="date">After June 4</span>
      <h2>Aftermath</h2>
      <p>The government launches arrests of protest leaders and participants, imposes strict information controls, and consolidates hardline leadership. Discussion of the events remains heavily censored within mainland China.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

  <section class="casualties">
    <h2>Estimated number of victims</h2>
    <p>No definitive death toll exists. The Chinese government has restricted investigation and released only limited, shifting figures, while independent researchers have produced widely varying estimates:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chinese government (later official statements):</strong> roughly <span class="estimate">200–300</span> deaths, including soldiers and civilians, with claims that no one was killed inside the square itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Chinese Red Cross (unofficial, 1989):</strong> estimates reported at the time of around <span class="estimate">2,600</span> dead — a figure the organization later said it could not confirm.</li>
      <li><strong>Student groups and dissident sources:</strong> claims of <span class="estimate">several thousand</span> deaths, generally considered unverifiable by outside researchers.</li>
      <li><strong>Western intelligence and diplomatic assessments (declassified):</strong> mostly in the range of <span class="estimate">several hundred to over 1,000</span>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Most scholarly and journalistic references therefore cite a range of roughly <strong>several hundred to several thousand</strong> civilian deaths, with the true figure likely unknowable. Thousands more were reportedly arrested in the weeks that followed.</p>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p>Prepared for educational reference. Figures are drawn from encyclopedic and declassified-documentary sources.</p>
    <p>Further reading: <a href="https://www.britannica.com/event/Tiananmen-Square-incident">Britannica — Tiananmen Square incident</a> · <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1989_Tiananmen_Square_protests_and_massacre">Wikipedia — 1989 Tiananmen Square protests and massacre</a></p>
  </footer>

</main>
</body>
</html>
```

A few notes on the choices made here:

- **Tone and framing**: The page uses neutral, factual language throughout ("protests," "crackdown," "students," "troops") and avoids inflammatory phrasing, since this is a sensitive historical topic.
- **Casualty figures**: Because the death toll is genuinely disputed, the page presents a *range of attributed estimates* (government, Red Cross, student groups, Western intelligence) rather than a single number, and explicitly states that no definitive toll exists. This is the academically honest approach.
- **Design**: A single-file page with an embedded dark theme, a vertical timeline with markers (red markers highlight the crackdown dates), and a dedicated casualties section. No JavaScript or external dependencies — it works offline by simply opening the file.

