<body>

<header class="wrap">
  <div class="kicker">Historical Record · Spring 1989</div>
  <h1>The Tiananmen Square Events<br>April – June 1989</h1>
  <p class="sub">A chronology of the student-led demonstrations in Beijing and the military crackdown of June 3–4, with a summary of casualty estimates.</p>
</header>

<main class="wrap">

  <p class="intro">
    In the spring of 1989, students and citizens gathered in Tiananmen Square and cities across China,
    calling for political reform, an end to corruption, and greater freedom of expression.
    The demonstrations ended when the government declared martial law and the People's Liberation Army
    moved into central Beijing. The events remain one of the most studied — and contested — episodes of the late twentieth century.
  </p>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 15, 1989</div>
      <h2>Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, the former General Secretary of the Chinese Communist Party and a widely respected
      reformist, dies of a heart attack. Students begin gathering in Tiananmen Square to mourn him and,
      voicing sympathy for his reformist legacy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 18 – 22</div>
      <h2>Mass demonstrations begin</h2>
      <p>Tens of thousands of students march to the square. On April 22, the day of Hu's memorial service,
      roughly 100,000 students gather and petition the Great Hall of the People. Premier Li Peng meets student
      representatives; the meeting is tense and unproductive.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 26</div>
      <h2>The "turmoil" editorial</h2>
      <p>A <em>People's Daily</em> editorial denounces the protests as "turmoil" orchestrated by a small group of
      agitators. Rather than deterring protesters, the editorial galvanizes them, and support for the movement spreads.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 4</div>
      <h2>May Fourth anniversary</h2>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, students and supporters march in Beijing
      and dozens of other cities. Demonstrations of solidarity spread to Shanghai, Guangzhou, and elsewhere.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 13</div>
      <h2>Hunger strike begins</h2>
      <p>Several thousand students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, demanding dialogue with the government.
      General Secretary Zhao Ziyang meets with strikers and expresses sympathy for their aims.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 15 – 18</div>
      <h2>Gorbachev visit</h2>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing for the Sino-Soviet summit — the first such summit in 30 years.
      The square remains occupied by protesters, and planned welcoming ceremonies are disrupted.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 19</div>
      <h2>Martial law announced</h2>
      <p>Li Peng announces martial law. In the early hours of May 20, Zhao Ziyang makes a tearful public appearance
      in the square, pleading with students to leave — his last public appearance before being sidelined.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 20 – June 2</div>
      <h2>Standoff in the square</h2>
      <p>Martial law is declared across Beijing, but residents and students block troop movements into the city.
      Estimates of the crowd in central Beijing at the peak reach as high as one million. The occupation of the
      square continues, with students erecting the "Goddess of Democracy" statue on May 30.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event crackdown">
      <div class="date">June 3 – 4</div>
      <h2>The crackdown</h2>
      <p>In the evening of June 3, troops begin moving into the city. Clashes erupt between soldiers and civilians
      around the city. In the early hours of June 4, the military clears Tiananmen Square, ending the
      seven-week occupation. The operation is followed by mass arrests, and martial law remains in force until January 1990.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 5</div>
      <h2>"Tank Man"</h2>
      <p>Along Chang'an Avenue, a lone man in a white shirt famously stands in front of a column of tanks,
      halting it briefly before being pulled away. The image becomes one of the most iconic photographs of the century.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

  <section class="casualties">
    <h2>Estimated number of victims</h2>
    <p class="note">No definitive figure exists. The Chinese government has never released a full accounting,
    and estimates vary enormously depending on source. Figures below are those most commonly cited.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Source</th><th>Estimate</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Chinese government (official)</td>
          <td class="fig">~200 – 300</td>
          <td>Includes civilians and soldiers killed; Beijing's stated figure for deaths citywide.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Chinese Red Cross (1989, later retracted)</td>
          <td class="fig">~2,600</td>
          <td>An early figure attributed to Red Cross workers, later disavowed under pressure.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Western media / diplomats (June 1989)</td>
          <td class="fig">"Thousands"</td>
          <td>Initial on-the-ground reports ranged from hundreds to over 10,000; many were later revised downward.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Declassified UK documents (released 2017)</td>
          <td class="fig">≥ 10,000</td>
          <td>Cited an internal Chinese estimate; its authenticity has been disputed by some analysts.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Scholarly consensus (e.g., Columbia, Princeton studies)</td>
          <td class="fig">Several hundred to several thousand</td>
          <td>Most researchers treat the precise toll as unknowable; deaths occurred across Beijing, not only in the square.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="range">
      <strong>In short:</strong> the widely accepted range is from <strong>several hundred to several thousand dead</strong>,
      with the true figure likely unknowable. Most independent researchers believe the death toll was concentrated
      in the streets around the square rather than inside it, and that it included both protesters and soldiers.
    </p>
  </section>

</main>

<footer class="wrap">
  <p>Prepared as a neutral historical summary. Casualty figures remain disputed and are presented as a range of estimates.</p>
  <p>Sources commonly consulted: Encyclopaedia Britannica · Columbia University "Tiananmen Square" archive · declassified UK Foreign Office documents (2017) · contemporaneous press reporting (June 1989).</p>
</footer>

<script>
  // Gentle reveal-on-scroll for timeline entries
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach(e => {
      if (e.isIntersecting) {
        e.target.classList.add('visible');
        observer.unobserve(e.target);
      }
    });
  }, { threshold: 0.15 });
  document.querySelectorAll('.event').forEach(el => observer.observe(el));
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

A few notes on the approach I took:

- **Neutral, encyclopedic tone** — the page presents the events factually (dates, key actors, sequence of events) without editorializing, similar to how a reference work would cover them.
- **Casualty figures handled carefully** — since the death toll is genuinely disputed, the page includes a dedicated section presenting the range of estimates by source (official Chinese figure, Red Cross figure, Western reports, the 2017 declassified UK documents, scholarly consensus) with brief context for each, plus an explicit note that no definitive figure exists.
- **Design** — a single self-contained file with a vertical timeline, a muted/documentary color palette (cream background, deep red accent), serif typography, and a subtle scroll-reveal animation that degrades gracefully without JavaScript.

If you'd like, I can adjust the tone (more academic, more journalistic), add a map, include a section on the aftermath and legacy, or translate it into another language.

