<body>

<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>The Tiananmen Square Protests</h1>
    <p class="sub">A timeline of the student-led demonstrations in Beijing and the military crackdown, April&nbsp;–&nbsp;June&nbsp;1989</p>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="wrap">

  <div class="intro">
    In the spring of 1989, students, workers, and citizens gathered in Beijing's Tiananmen
    Square — initially to mourn the death of reformist party leader Hu Yaobang, and soon to
    demand democratic reform, an end to corruption, and greater freedom of the press. After
    seven weeks of protests and a failed attempt at dialogue, the government declared
    martial law and sent in the army. The crackdown on the night of June 3–4, 1989 made
    the events known around the world.
  </div>

  <section class="stats">
    <h2>Estimated Number of Victims</h2>
    <p class="note">The death toll remains disputed; no definitive figure exists.</p>
    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="num">~200–300</span>
        <span class="label">Dead, according to the Chinese government's official account (including soldiers)</span>
        <span class="src">PRC official figures, 1989; ~7,000 wounded also reported officially</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="num">Hundreds – ~1,000+</span>
        <span class="label">Dead, per Western diplomatic and intelligence estimates made at the time</span>
        <span class="src">U.S. Embassy / Western diplomatic reporting, June 1989</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="num">Up to several thousand</span>
        <span class="label">Dead, per some independent researchers and later analyses</span>
        <span class="src">Various academic and documentary studies</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="disclaimer">
      ⚠ Figures of 10,000 or more have circulated, but are not supported by most
      contemporary evidence; a declassified British diplomatic report (2017) concluded
      such numbers were likely exaggerated. The exact toll is unknown, as many deaths
      went unrecorded and records remain sealed.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="timeline">
    <h2>Key Dates</h2>
    <div class="tl">

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">Apr 15, 1989</span>
        <h3>Death of Hu Yaobang</h3>
        <p>The former General Secretary of the Communist Party, a reformist purged in 1987
        for being "soft" on student protests, dies of a heart attack. Students at Beijing
        universities begin posting mourning posters — grief quickly turns political.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">Apr 18, 1989</span>
        <h3>First mass march to the Square</h3>
        <p>Thousands of students march to Tiananmen Square, chanting for democracy,
        against corruption, and for freedom of the press. Similar gatherings spread to
        other cities.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">Apr 22, 1989</span>
        <h3>Day of the memorial service</h3>
        <p>Roughly 100,000 students gather in the Square for Hu Yaobang's memorial service.
        Student representatives try to deliver a petition to Premier Li Peng but are refused
        a meeting.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">Apr 26, 1989</span>
        <h3>The "turmoil" editorial</h3>
        <p>A <em>People's Daily</em> editorial denounces the movement as a "well-planned plot"
        and "turmoil." Instead of intimidating students, it enrages them and swells the
        protests.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">May 4, 1989</span>
        <h3>May Fourth anniversary</h3>
        <p>Tens of thousands march on the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement,
        a historic student protest. Demonstrations also take place in Shanghai, Guangzhou,
        and other cities.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">May 13, 1989</span>
        <h3>Hunger strike begins</h3>
        <p>More than a thousand students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square. Support
        grows nationwide as workers, teachers, and ordinary citizens join the demonstrations.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">May 15–18, 1989</span>
        <h3>Gorbachev's summit overshadowed</h3>
        <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing for the first Sino-Soviet summit in
        30 years. The protests dominate international coverage. Party chief Zhao Ziyang meets
        Gorbachev, publicly revealing a split in the leadership.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">May 19, 1989</span>
        <h3>Martial law declared</h3>
        <p>Premier Li Peng announces martial law. In the early hours of May 20, Zhao Ziyang
        makes a tearful, televised plea to students to leave the Square — his last public
        appearance before being purged.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">May 20–23, 1989</span>
        <h3>Citizens block the troops</h3>
        <p>Martial law takes effect, but hundreds of thousands of Beijing residents surround
        army convoys and block their advance for days. Soldiers, many unarmed and from rural
        areas, fraternize with the crowds.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">May 23, 1989</span>
        <h3>Million-person march</h3>
        <p>An estimated one million or more people march through Beijing demanding Li Peng's
        resignation — one of the largest demonstrations in the city's history.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">May 30, 1989</span>
        <h3>"Goddess of Democracy" unveiled</h3>
        <p>Art students erect a 10-metre papier-mâché statue, the "Goddess of Democracy,"
        in the Square, facing Mao Zedong's portrait on Tiananmen Gate.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event crackdown">
        <span class="date">Jun 3–4, 1989</span>
        <h3>The crackdown</h3>
        <p>On the night of June 3–4, troops armed with live ammunition move into central
        Beijing. Heavy shooting occurs around Muxidi and along Chang'an Avenue. By dawn on
        June 4, soldiers have cleared Tiananmen Square. This is the event's deadliest
        phase, and the source of most casualty estimates.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event crackdown">
        <span class="date">Jun 4, 1989</span>
        <h3>"Tank Man"</h3>
        <p>On the morning of June 4, a lone man in a white shirt famously stands in front of
        a column of tanks on Chang'an Avenue, blocking its path. The image becomes one of
        the most iconic photographs of the 20th century.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="event">
        <span class="date">Aftermath</span>
        <h3>Arrests and purge</h3>
        <p>Thousands are arrested across China. Zhao Ziyang is removed from power and placed
        under house arrest. Protests in Shanghai and other cities are also suppressed.
        Martial law remains in force in Beijing until January 1990.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>

<footer>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>Prepared for educational purposes. Casualty figures are estimates and remain
    historically disputed; sources include official PRC statements, contemporary
    Western diplomatic reporting, and later academic research.</p>
    <p>Events of June 3–4, 1989 are commemorated annually in Hong Kong and by vigils
    around the world.</p>
  </div>
</footer>

</body>
</html>
```

This page includes:

- **A header and introduction** summarizing the context of the spring 1989 protests.
- **A "Victims" section** presenting the range of death-toll estimates side by side — the official PRC figure (~200–300), Western diplomatic estimates (hundreds to ~1,000+), and higher independent claims — with an explicit disclaimer that the exact toll is unknown and that figures of 10,000+ are not corroborated by most evidence.
- **A 14-entry vertical timeline** from Hu Yaobang's death (April 15) through the crackdown (June 3–4) and aftermath, with the two June 3–4 events visually highlighted.
- **A clean, responsive design** with a single accent color, a timeline rail with date markers, and print-friendly styling — all in one self-contained file with no external dependencies.

