    th, td { padding: 10px 8px; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <div class="candle">
      <svg width="28" height="40" viewBox="0 0 28 40" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M14 2 C17 7 19 10 19 13.5 A5 5 0 0 1 9 13.5 C9 10 11 7 14 2 Z" fill="#c9a227"/>
        <rect x="12.5" y="19" width="3" height="6" fill="#e8e6e1"/>
        <rect x="6" y="25" width="16" height="12" rx="2" fill="#3a3a38" stroke="#c9a227" stroke-width="1"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <h1>Tiananmen Square, 1989</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A timeline of the pro-democracy demonstrations in Beijing and the military crackdown of June 3–4</p>
    <span class="range-badge">April 15 — June 1989</span>
  </header>

  <p class="intro">
    In the spring of 1989, students and citizens gathered in Beijing's Tiananmen Square — and in hundreds
    of cities across China — to mourn the death of former Communist Party leader Hu Yaobang and to call for
    political reform, freedom of the press, and an end to corruption. Over seven weeks the demonstrations grew.
    On the night of June 3–4, the government ordered the People's Liberation Army to clear the square, and a
    violent crackdown followed. The events remain one of the most contested episodes of the late twentieth century.
  </p>

  <h2>Timeline of key events</h2>

  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 15, 1989</div>
      <h3>Death of Hu Yaobang</h3>
      <p>The former General Secretary of the Communist Party, a reformist popular with students and intellectuals, dies. Spontaneous mourning begins at universities in Beijing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 17–18</div>
      <h3>First gatherings in the Square</h3>
      <p>Students march to Tiananmen Square to lay wreaths. Slogans calling for democracy, anti-corruption measures, and press freedom appear for the first time.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 22</div>
      <h3>Memorial service</h3>
      <p>On the day of Hu Yaobang's official memorial service, an estimated 100,000 students gather in the square. A group presents a petition to the National People's Congress.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 26</div>
      <h3>The People's Daily editorial</h3>
      <p>A People's Daily editorial denounces the movement as a "well-planned plot" to cause "turmoil." Rather than intimidating protesters, the editorial galvanizes larger demonstrations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 4</div>
      <h3>May Fourth anniversary</h3>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, hundreds of thousands march in Beijing, Shanghai, and dozens of other cities.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 13–18</div>
      <h3>Hunger strike; Gorbachev's visit</h3>
      <p>Thousands of students begin a hunger strike in the square. Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing (May 15–18), and the Sino-Soviet summit is overshadowed by the protests.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 19</div>
      <h3>Martial law declared</h3>
      <p>Party leaders declare martial law in Beijing. Party General Secretary Zhao Ziyang makes an emotional televised plea to the students to end the hunger strike, in his final public appearance.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 20–June 2</div>
      <h3>Troops blocked by crowds</h3>
      <p>Martial law takes effect, but tens of thousands of Beijing residents block army convoys from entering the city. Soldiers and civilians fraternize; the square remains occupied.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 30</div>
      <h3>"Goddess of Democracy"</h3>
      <p>Art students unveil a 10-meter statue, the "Goddess of Democracy," in the square facing the portrait of Mao Zedong on Tiananmen Gate.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event crackdown">
      <div class="date">June 3–4</div>
      <h3>The crackdown</h3>
      <p>In the evening of June 3, troops with live ammunition move into Beijing. Overnight and into the early morning of June 4, soldiers clear Tiananmen Square and fire on crowds and barricades across the city. Hundreds — possibly thousands — are killed.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 5</div>
      <h3>"Tank Man"</h3>
      <p>A lone man in a white shirt famously stands in front of a column of tanks on Chang'an Avenue, becoming an enduring image of individual resistance.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 1989 – January 1990</div>
      <h3>Aftermath</h3>
      <p>Thousands are arrested; protest leaders are imprisoned or sent into exile. Zhao Ziyang is purged from the Party. Martial law is lifted in Beijing on January 10, 1990.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Estimated number of victims</h2>
  <p style="color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    No definitive death toll exists. The Chinese government has never released a complete accounting, and
    independent investigation has been restricted. Estimates vary widely:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Source</th><th>Estimate</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Government of the PRC (official statements, 1989)</td>
        <td class="num">~200–300</td>
        <td>Including soldiers and police killed</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Chinese Red Cross (figure reported at the time; later disavowed by the organization)</td>
        <td class="num">~2,600</td>
        <td>Reported by some outlets in June 1989</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Student groups / <em>The Tiananmen Papers</em></td>
        <td class="num">3,000+</td>
        <td>Compiled from hospital and morgue reports</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Declassified Western intelligence estimates (1989–2017)</td>
        <td class="num">Several hundred – ~1,000</td>
        <td>Based on reports available at the time</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Human rights organizations</td>
        <td class="num">1,000+</td>
        <td>Often citing unverified morgue and hospital figures</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Wounded:</strong> Official Chinese figures cited roughly 7,000–9,000 injured; independent estimates are
    higher. Most scholars today describe the death toll as <strong>several hundred to several thousand</strong>,
    with the true figure unknown. The victims included students, workers, residents, and soldiers.
  </div>

  <footer>
    <p>Compiled from publicly available historical sources, including encyclopedic references, declassified
    documents, and human rights reports. Figures remain disputed and are presented as reported by each source.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 8px;">In memory of those who died, June 1989.</p>
  </footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
```

**Implementation notes:**

- **Design** — A dark, restrained, memorial-like aesthetic: charcoal background, serif headings (Source Serif 4) with a sans body (Inter), a single muted red accent used only for timeline markers (filled for the June 3–4 crackdown entry), and a small candle SVG at the top.
- **Timeline** — A vertical rail with dot markers; each event has an uppercase gold date label, a serif heading, and a one-to-two-sentence neutral summary covering April 15 (Hu Yaobang's death) through the aftermath (martial law lifted January 1990).
- **Victims section** — Rather than presenting one number as fact, it shows a table of estimates attributed to each source (PRC government, Chinese Red Cross, student compilations, declassified intelligence, human rights groups), plus a note explaining why the true toll is unknown. This reflects the genuine historical dispute.
- **Responsive** — Single-column layout that adapts down to mobile widths; no JavaScript or external assets beyond the Google Fonts link.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="zh-CN">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>1989年春夏之交北京事件时间线</title>
    <style>
        * {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }

        body {
            font-family: "Microsoft YaHei", "SimSun", sans-serif;
