<body>

<header>
  <div class="kicker">Historical Record · Spring 1989</div>
  <h1>Tiananmen Square</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A timeline of the pro-democracy demonstrations in Beijing and the military crackdown of June 3–4, 1989.</p>
  <div class="date-range">April 15 — June 5, 1989</div>
</header>

<section class="intro">
  <p>In the spring of 1989, students, workers, and citizens gathered in <strong>Tiananmen Square</strong> and cities across China to call for political reform, press freedom, and an end to corruption. After weeks of escalating tension, the government declared martial law and, on the night of <strong>June 3–4</strong>, deployed the People's Liberation Army to clear the square. The exact number of deaths remains disputed; this page presents the publicly available estimates.</p>
</section>

<section class="timeline-section">
  <h2 class="section-title">Key Dates</h2>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">April 15, 1989</div>
      <h3>Death of Hu Yaobang</h3>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, a former Communist Party general secretary known for his reformist sympathies, dies. Students gather in Beijing to mourn him — mourning quickly turns into calls for political change.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">April 18 – 22</div>
      <h3>Protests begin and spread</h3>
      <p>Thousands of students march to Tiananmen Square. Similar demonstrations erupt in Shanghai, Xi'an, Changsha, and other cities. On April 22, an estimated 100,000 students gather in the square.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">April 26</div>
      <h3>The People's Daily editorial</h3>
      <p>A <em>People's Daily</em> editorial denounces the protests as "turmoil" orchestrated by a small group. Rather than quelling the movement, the editorial triggers larger demonstrations across the country.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">April 27</div>
      <h3>Mass demonstration</h3>
      <p>An estimated one million people — students, workers, and residents — march through Beijing in one of the largest gatherings of the movement.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">May 4</div>
      <h3>May Fourth anniversary</h3>
      <p>Marking the 70th anniversary of the 1919 May Fourth Movement, students and intellectuals march again, framing their demands within China's own history of patriotic reform.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">May 13</div>
      <h3>Hunger strike begins</h3>
      <p>Roughly 3,000 students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, raising the stakes and drawing enormous public sympathy. Support vigils spread nationwide.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">May 15 – 18</div>
      <h3>Gorbachev's visit</h3>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev arrives for the Sino-Soviet summit — the first such visit in 30 years. The protests overshadow the diplomatic event and embarrass the government on the world stage.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">May 19</div>
      <h3>Martial law declared</h3>
      <p>Premier Li Peng announces martial law. Party leader Zhao Ziyang makes a tearful public plea to the students to leave the square — his last public appearance; he is soon removed from power.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="event-date">May 20 – June 2</div>
      <h3>Troops blocked by crowds</h3>
      <p>Martial law takes effect, but tens of thousands of Beijing residents and students block army convoys from reaching the city center. The military temporarily withdraws. Crowds estimated at over a million fill the streets.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event crackdown">
      <div class="event-date">Night of June 3 – 4</div>
      <h3>The crackdown</h3>
      <p>In the evening of June 3, soldiers and armored vehicles move into Beijing. Troops open fire on crowds and students near the square. By dawn on June 4, the army has cleared Tiananmen Square. Fighting and shootings continue in surrounding neighborhoods into June 4.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event crackdown">
      <div class="event-date">June 4 – 5</div>
      <h3>Aftermath</h3>
      <p>On the morning of June 5, a lone man briefly stands before a column of tanks on Chang'an Avenue — an image that becomes known worldwide as "Tank Man." In the following weeks, thousands are arrested; many protest leaders receive long prison sentences.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="toll-section">
  <div class="toll-inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Estimated Death Toll</h2>
    <p class="toll-intro">No definitive figure exists. The Chinese government has never released a verified account, and the event is censored in mainland China. The estimates below come from different sources and vary by more than an order of magnitude.</p>

    <div class="toll-grid">
      <div class="toll-card">
        <div class="toll-number">~300</div>
        <div class="toll-source">Official PRC figure</div>
        <div class="toll-note">The Chinese government's State Council statement reported roughly 200–300 deaths, including soldiers and civilians.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="toll-card">
        <div class="toll-number">~2,600</div>
        <div class="toll-source">Chinese Red Cross</div>
        <div class="toll-note">An initial figure cited by the Red Cross in Beijing, later withdrawn under pressure, according to later reports.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="toll-card">
        <div class="toll-number">Hundreds – thousands</div>
        <div class="toll-source">Independent researchers</div>
        <div class="toll-note">Western journalists, human rights organizations, and academic studies have produced estimates ranging from several hundred to several thousand killed.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="toll-card">
        <div class="toll-number">10,000+</div>
        <div class="toll-source">Declassified UK documents</div>
        <div class="toll-note">British diplomatic cables declassified in 2017 cited a Chinese source claiming at least 10,000 deaths.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="toll-disclaimer">
      <strong>Note:</strong> The true death toll is unknown and likely unknowable. Figures include both civilians and soldiers, and definitions of who was killed — and where — differ between accounts. Most independent assessments place civilian deaths in the hundreds to low thousands.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="context">
  <h2>Context &amp; Further Reading</h2>
  <p>The events of spring 1989 remain one of the most sensitive topics in modern Chinese history. Discussion of the crackdown is censored in mainland China, and public commemoration is not permitted there. Annual vigils have long been held in Hong Kong (until 2020) and by diaspora communities worldwide.</p>
  <p>This page is a brief educational summary. For deeper study, the following types of sources are commonly consulted:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Academic histories of the 1989 democracy movement (e.g., works by historians of modern China)</li>
    <li>Reports by human rights organizations such as Amnesty International and Human Rights Watch</li>
    <li>Declassified government documents from the UK, US, and other countries, released in recent years</li>
    <li>Eyewitness journalism from Beijing, June 1989, and subsequent documentary investigations</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Figures and dates on this page reflect the mainstream historical consensus as of its writing. Where accounts conflict, the range of estimates is presented rather than a single number.</p>
</section>

<footer>
  Educational summary · Compiled from publicly available historical sources · Figures marked "~" are estimates
</footer>

<script>
  // Reveal timeline events as they scroll into view
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach(entry => {
      if (entry.isIntersecting) {
        entry.target.classList.add('visible');
        observer.unobserve(entry.target);
      }
    });
  }, { threshold: 0.15 });

  document.querySelectorAll('.event').forEach(el => observer.observe(el));
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

This page presents the events of April–June 1989 as a neutral, educational timeline. A few notes on the choices I made:

- **Factual framing**: All entries reflect the established historical record — Hu Yaobang's death sparking the protests, the hunger strikes, Gorbachev's visit, martial law, the June 3–4 crackdown, and the "Tank Man" incident.
- **Death toll honesty**: Since no verified figure exists, I presented a range across four source categories (official PRC, Chinese Red Cross, independent researchers, and declassified UK documents) rather than a single number, with a disclaimer explaining the uncertainty.
- **Design**: A dark, solemn aesthetic with a central vertical timeline (collapsing to a left-aligned line on mobile), scroll-reveal animations, and highlighted markers on the two crackdown events. It's fully responsive and print-friendly.
- **Context section**: Notes the ongoing censorship in mainland China and points readers toward further sources without linking to any single partisan account.

You can save it as an `.html` file and open it in any browser — no dependencies or build step required.

Here's a single-file HTML page presenting an educational timeline of the 1989 Tiananmen Square protests and massacre, with attributed death toll estimates:

