</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <h1>The Tiananmen Square Protests &amp; Crackdown</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A timeline of events in Beijing, April – June 1989</p>
  </header>

  <div class="timeline">

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 15, 1989</div>
      <h2>Death of Hu Yaobang</h2>
      <p>Hu Yaobang, a reformist former Communist Party leader popular with students and intellectuals, dies of a heart attack. His death sparks widespread mourning, and students begin gathering in Beijing to commemorate him — and to raise broader calls for political reform.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 18 – 22</div>
      <h2>Marches to Tiananmen Square</h2>
      <p>Thousands of students march to Tiananmen Square, where mourning gatherings grow into sustained demonstrations. Protesters call for an end to corruption, greater democracy, freedom of the press, and dialogue with the government. Similar protests spread to Shanghai, Xi'an, and other cities.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">April 26</div>
      <h2>"Turmoil" Editorial</h2>
      <p>A <em>People's Daily</em> editorial denounces the protests as a "planned conspiracy" and "turmoil" that must be opposed. Rather than quelling the movement, the editorial hardens protesters' resolve and draws larger crowds.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">Early May</div>
      <h2>Protests Peak</h2>
      <p>At the height of the movement, an estimated one million people gather in Tiananmen Square. Workers, journalists, and citizens from many walks of life join the students. The movement remains largely peaceful and leaderless.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 13</div>
      <h2>Hunger Strike Begins</h2>
      <p>More than 3,000 students begin a hunger strike in the square, timed to coincide with the upcoming Sino-Soviet summit. Their sacrifice draws enormous public sympathy across China.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 15 – 18</div>
      <h2>Gorbachev Visit</h2>
      <p>Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev visits Beijing for a Sino-Soviet summit — the first in 30 years. Party leader Zhao Ziyang meets with students sympathetically, and the visit's worldwide media coverage embarrasses the government, which is forced to move the welcome ceremony away from the square.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 19</div>
      <h2>Martial Law Declared</h2>
      <p>Zhao Ziyang makes a tearful, televised plea to students in the square to end the hunger strike. That evening, the government declares martial law in Beijing. Zhao is soon removed from power.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">May 20</div>
      <h2>Troops Blocked</h2>
      <p>Martial law takes formal effect. Tens of thousands of unarmed Beijing residents — students, workers, and ordinary citizens — block army convoys from entering the city center, fraternizing with soldiers and persuading many units to turn back.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event highlight">
      <div class="date">Night of June 3 – June 4</div>
      <h2>The Crackdown</h2>
      <p>In the early hours of June 4, the military moves into central Beijing with tanks and live ammunition to clear the square. Gunfire and violence erupt across the city, mostly west of the square. Troops fire on crowds and makeshift barricades; civilians are killed and wounded in large numbers. By morning, the square has been cleared.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 5</div>
      <h2>"Tank Man"</h2>
      <p>A lone, unidentified man in a white shirt famously stands in front of a column of tanks on Chang'an Avenue, blocking their advance. He is pulled away by bystanders. The image, captured by photographers, becomes one of the most iconic photographs of the 20th century. His identity and fate remain unknown.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="event">
      <div class="date">June 9</div>
      <h2>Aftermath</h2>
      <p>Deng Xiaoping praises the military's actions in a televised address, framing the crackdown as necessary to stop "counter-revolutionary turmoil." A nationwide arrest campaign follows, and many protest leaders are imprisoned or flee abroad. Discussion of the events is suppressed inside China in the years that follow.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

  <section class="toll">
    <h2>Estimated Death Toll</h2>
    <p>The exact number of people killed remains disputed and unverifiable. Estimates vary widely:</p>
    <span class="figure">Hundreds to several thousand</span>
    <p><strong>Chinese government (official):</strong> roughly 200–300 deaths, including soldiers.</p>
    <p><strong>Chinese Red Cross (at the time, later retracted):</strong> an initial estimate of about 2,600 civilian deaths.</p>
    <p><strong>Western governments &amp; human rights organizations (contemporary estimates):</strong> several hundred to over 1,000.</p>
    <p><strong>Declassified documents (UK, released 2017):</strong> suggested a figure of at least 10,000 killed — a figure historians have treated with caution.</p>
    <p>No definitive toll exists. Most independent researchers today place the number of civilian deaths in the <strong>hundreds to low thousands</strong> range.</p>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p><strong>A note on sources:</strong> This timeline draws on established historical accounts, including works by historians such as Jonathan Spence, Timothy Brook, and the National Security Archive's declassified document collections. Casualty figures remain contested; the range above reflects the spread of credible estimates.</p>
    <p>This page is a factual historical summary. The events of June 4, 1989 are commemorated annually around the world, though public discussion of them is restricted in mainland China.</p>
  </footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
```

This page presents the timeline as a clean vertical layout with dated event cards, a highlighted section for the June 3–4 crackdown, and a dedicated death-toll panel that honestly presents the range of estimates (official Chinese figures vs. Red Cross, Western, and declassified-document estimates) since no definitive number exists. Save it as an `.html` file and open it in any browser.

