            color: #777;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Tiananmen Square Massacre Timeline</h1>
    <p><em>April–June 1989: A pro-democracy movement in Beijing, China, and its violent suppression by the government.</em></p>

    <div class="timeline">
        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">April 15, 1989</span>
            <p>The death of former Communist Party General Secretary <strong>Hu Yaobang</strong>, a reformist leader, sparks student gatherings in Tiananmen Square to mourn and call for political reforms.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">April 17–22, 1989</span>
            <p>Thousands of students from Beijing universities march to Tiananmen Square, demanding greater freedom of speech, a free press, and democratic reforms. The movement spreads to other cities.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">April 26, 1989</span>
            <p>The <em>People's Daily</em> publishes an editorial labeling the protests as "turmoil" (<em>dongluan</em>), escalating tensions. Students respond with larger demonstrations.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">May 4, 1989</span>
            <p>Over 100,000 students and workers march in Beijing, commemorating the 70th anniversary of the May Fourth Movement (1919) and demanding dialogue with the government.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">May 13, 1989</span>
            <p>Students begin a <strong>hunger strike</strong> in Tiananmen Square, drawing massive public support. An estimated <strong>1 million</strong> Beijing residents join protests in solidarity.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">May 15, 1989</span>
            <p>Soviet leader <strong>Mikhail Gorbachev</strong> visits Beijing. The government, fearing embarrassment, declares martial law, but troops are blocked by protesters from entering the city.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">May 19–20, 1989</span>
            <p>Premier <strong>Li Peng</strong> declares martial law, and the government deploys <strong>250,000 troops</strong> to Beijing. Citizens and students set up barricades to block the military.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">June 3–4, 1989</span>
            <p>The <strong>People's Liberation Army (PLA)</strong> advances on Tiananmen Square with tanks and armed soldiers. Protesters resist, but the military opens fire on civilians in the streets leading to the square.</p>
            <p><strong>Massacre begins:</strong> Troops clear the square by early morning on June 4, crushing the protests with lethal force.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">June 4, 1989</span>
            <p>The government declares the protests "counter-revolutionary." The iconic <strong>"Tank Man"</strong> image is captured as an unidentified man stands before a column of tanks.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">June 5–6, 1989</span>
            <p>Mass arrests begin. The government conducts a brutal crackdown, detaining thousands, executing some, and forcing others into exile.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="death-toll">
            <h2>Estimated Death Toll</h2>
            <p><strong>Official Chinese government figure:</strong> 241 dead (including soldiers), ~7,000 wounded.</p>
            <p><strong>Independent estimates:</strong> 2,000–10,000+ civilians killed (Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch, and eyewitness accounts). The true number remains disputed due to government censorship.</p>
            <p><strong>Aftermath:</strong> Thousands imprisoned, many executed or "disappeared." The event remains heavily censored in China today.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <footer>
        <p>Sources: Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch, <em>The Tiananmen Papers</em>, and historical accounts.</p>
        <p>Last updated: 2023</p>
    </footer>
</body>
</html>
```

