    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Tiananmen Square Massacre Timeline (1989)</h1>

    <p>The Tiananmen Square protests were a series of student-led demonstrations in Beijing, China, calling for democracy, free speech, and government accountability. The protests were violently suppressed by the Chinese government in June 1989.</p>

    <div class="timeline">
        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">April 15, 1989</span>
            <p>Death of former Communist Party General Secretary Hu Yaobang, a reformist leader, sparks mourning and protests among students in Beijing.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">April 17–18, 1989</span>
            <p>Thousands of students gather in Tiananmen Square to demand democratic reforms and greater political freedom. Protests spread to other cities.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">April 22, 1989</span>
            <p>Hu Yaobang's funeral is held. Over 100,000 students and citizens march in Beijing, defying government warnings.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">April 26, 1989</span>
            <p>The <em>People's Daily</em> publishes an editorial condemning the protests as "turmoil," escalating tensions.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">May 4, 1989</span>
            <p>Massive student demonstrations continue, with protesters calling for dialogue with the government. Hunger strikes begin in Tiananmen Square.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">May 13, 1989</span>
            <p>Students begin a hunger strike in Tiananmen Square, drawing widespread public sympathy. Millions of Beijing residents join the protests.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">May 19, 1989</span>
            <p>Premier Li Peng declares martial law, and troops begin moving toward Beijing. Citizens block the army's advance.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">June 3–4, 1989</span>
            <p>The Chinese government sends troops and tanks to clear Tiananmen Square. Violent clashes erupt across Beijing. Protesters are shot, beaten, and arrested. The square is cleared by June 4.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="event">
            <span class="date">June 4, 1989 (and following days)</span>
            <p>Mass arrests, executions, and purges of protesters and dissidents continue. The government imposes strict censorship and suppresses all mentions of the event.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="death-toll">
        <h2>Estimated Death Toll</h2>
        <p>The exact number of deaths remains disputed due to government censorship. Estimates range from:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Official Chinese government figure:</strong> 241 dead (including soldiers) and 7,000 wounded.</li>
            <li><strong>Human rights groups and witnesses:</strong> Hundreds to over 1,000 civilians killed in Beijing alone, with thousands more injured or arrested.</li>
            <li><strong>Other estimates:</strong> Some sources suggest up to 10,000 deaths nationwide, including executions and disappearances in the aftermath.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><em>Note:</em> The true scale of the massacre may never be known due to the Chinese government's ongoing suppression of information.</p>
    </div>

    <footer>
        <p>This timeline is a simplified overview. For more details, consult historical records and human rights organizations.</p>
    </footer>
